::CW_U01_L05_Q01::[MLA.CW.WR.10] Passage: "The receipt stayed folded in his wallet long after the gift was gone." What inference is best supported?{
~A. The receipt is newly printed.#Incorrect. It has stayed in the wallet long after the gift disappeared.
=B. The receipt may carry emotional meaning beyond the purchase.#Correct. Keeping it after the gift is gone suggests memory or attachment.
~C. The character hates paper.#Incorrect. Keeping the receipt does not suggest hatred of paper.
~D. The gift is still wrapped on the table.#Incorrect. The gift is gone.
}

::CW_U01_L05_Q02::[MLA.CW.WR.08] Passage: "The receipt stayed folded in his wallet long after the gift was gone." Which evidence best supports the inference that the receipt matters?{
~A. The word "wallet" appears.#Incorrect. The wallet alone does not prove importance.
~B. The sentence is short.#Incorrect. Sentence length is not the key evidence here.
~C. The gift was expensive.#Incorrect. Price is not mentioned.
=D. It stayed folded in his wallet long after the gift was gone.#Correct. Keeping it over time supports emotional importance.
}

::CW_U01_L05_Q03::[MLA.CW.WR.09] Passage: "The receipt stayed folded in his wallet long after the gift was gone." Which craft move does the writer use?{
=A. A small object implies a larger memory.#Correct. The receipt suggests meaning beyond its literal function.
~B. A direct explanation of every feeling.#Incorrect. Feelings are implied, not directly explained.
~C. Dialogue between two people.#Incorrect. No dialogue appears.
~D. A weather description.#Incorrect. Weather is not described.
}

::CW_U01_L05_Q04::[MLA.CW.WR.10] Passage: "Tessa turned the invitation over and over until the corners softened." What can the reader infer?{
~A. Tessa has no interest in the invitation.#Incorrect. Repeatedly handling it suggests interest or concern.
~B. Tessa has already thrown it away.#Incorrect. She is holding it.
=C. Tessa may be uncertain about the invitation.#Correct. Turning it over repeatedly suggests hesitation or thought.
~D. Tessa cannot read any words.#Incorrect. The passage does not support that.
}

::CW_U01_L05_Q05::[MLA.CW.WR.08] Passage: "Tessa turned the invitation over and over until the corners softened." Which detail is the strongest evidence of hesitation?{
=A. turned the invitation over and over#Correct. Repeated handling supports hesitation or uncertainty.
~B. invitation#Incorrect. The object alone does not show hesitation.
~C. corners#Incorrect. The corners matter because of what happens to them, not by themselves.
~D. Tessa#Incorrect. The name alone is not evidence.
}

::CW_U01_L05_Q06::[MLA.CW.WR.09] Passage: "Tessa turned the invitation over and over until the corners softened." Why might a writer choose this detail?{
~A. To explain how paper is manufactured.#Incorrect. The passage is not informational.
~B. To remove character emotion from the scene.#Incorrect. The detail implies emotion.
~C. To show that invitations cannot bend.#Incorrect. The corners soften, so it bends or wears.
=D. To imply uncertainty through action rather than stating it directly.#Correct. The action lets the reader infer Tessa's hesitation.
}

::CW_U01_L05_Q07::[MLA.CW.WR.10] Passage: "The classroom lights stayed on, but every desk was empty except hers." What inference is supported?{
~A. The classroom is crowded.#Incorrect. Every desk except hers is empty.
=B. The character may feel isolated.#Correct. One occupied desk among many empty desks suggests isolation.
~C. The lights are broken.#Incorrect. The lights stay on.
~D. The character is at a concert.#Incorrect. The setting is a classroom.
}

::CW_U01_L05_Q08::[MLA.CW.WR.08] Passage: "The classroom lights stayed on, but every desk was empty except hers." Which evidence supports the claim that the setting creates isolation?{
~A. classroom lights#Incorrect. Lights alone do not create isolation.
~B. stayed on#Incorrect. This does not show the character alone.
=C. every desk was empty except hers#Correct. This exact detail shows physical separation.
~D. The passage has a comma.#Incorrect. Punctuation alone is not evidence of isolation here.
}

::CW_U01_L05_Q09::[MLA.CW.WR.10] Passage: "The classroom lights stayed on, but every desk was empty except hers." Which inference goes too far?{
~A. The character is the only one at a desk.#Incorrect. This is directly supported.
~B. The room may feel lonely.#Incorrect. This is a reasonable inference.
~C. The setting may emphasize isolation.#Incorrect. This is supported by the empty desks.
=D. The character is secretly a famous actor.#Correct. Nothing in the passage supports this.
}

::CW_U01_L05_Q10::[MLA.CW.WR.09] Passage: "Ari deleted the message, then typed it again from memory." What craft choice creates meaning?{
=A. Repeated action with the same message#Correct. Deleting and retyping suggests conflict or obsession.
~B. A direct statement that Ari is happy#Incorrect. No direct emotion is stated.
~C. A description of the weather#Incorrect. Weather is absent.
~D. A list of phone brands#Incorrect. No brands are listed.
}

::CW_U01_L05_Q11::[MLA.CW.WR.10] Passage: "Ari deleted the message, then typed it again from memory." What can the reader infer?{
~A. Ari forgot the message completely.#Incorrect. Ari types it again from memory.
~B. Ari never had a message.#Incorrect. Ari deletes and retypes one.
=C. Ari may be unsure whether to send the message.#Correct. Deleting and retyping suggests hesitation.
~D. Ari is repairing a keyboard.#Incorrect. No repair is mentioned.
}

::CW_U01_L05_Q12::[MLA.CW.WR.08] Passage: "Ari deleted the message, then typed it again from memory." Which evidence best supports the inference of hesitation?{
~A. Ari's name#Incorrect. A name is not evidence of hesitation.
=B. deleted the message, then typed it again#Correct. The repeated reversal shows uncertainty.
~C. memory#Incorrect. Memory shows recall, not hesitation by itself.
~D. The sentence has one comma.#Incorrect. Punctuation is not the key evidence.
}

::CW_U01_L05_Q13::[MLA.CW.WR.10] Passage: "The front porch was swept clean except for one muddy footprint facing the door." What is a reasonable inference?{
=A. Someone may have arrived or returned unexpectedly.#Correct. The single muddy footprint facing the door suggests arrival.
~B. No one has ever been on the porch.#Incorrect. The footprint shows someone was there.
~C. The porch is underwater.#Incorrect. No water is described.
~D. The door is missing.#Incorrect. The footprint faces the door.
}

::CW_U01_L05_Q14::[MLA.CW.WR.09] Passage: "The front porch was swept clean except for one muddy footprint facing the door." Which detail makes the footprint important?{
~A. The passage names a roof.#Incorrect. No roof is named.
~B. The footprint is indoors.#Incorrect. It is on the porch.
~C. The sentence explains the full backstory.#Incorrect. The backstory is implied.
=D. The porch is otherwise swept clean.#Correct. The clean porch makes the one footprint stand out.
}

::CW_U01_L05_Q15::[MLA.CW.WR.08] Passage: "The front porch was swept clean except for one muddy footprint facing the door." Which claim is best supported?{
~A. The writer uses dialogue to reveal a secret.#Incorrect. There is no dialogue.
~B. The writer states the character is angry.#Incorrect. No emotion is directly stated.
=C. The writer uses contrast to make the footprint noticeable.#Correct. The clean porch contrasts with the muddy footprint.
~D. The writer describes a crowded party.#Incorrect. The porch has one footprint, not a crowd.
}

::CW_U01_L05_Q16::[MLA.CW.WR.10] Passage: "The birthday candle burned down while everyone watched the empty chair." What can the reader infer?{
~A. Everyone forgot there was a candle.#Incorrect. Everyone is watching.
=B. Someone expected is absent.#Correct. The empty chair at a birthday moment suggests absence.
~C. The chair is on fire.#Incorrect. The candle burns, not the chair.
~D. The birthday has already been canceled.#Incorrect. A candle is present.
}

::CW_U01_L05_Q17::[MLA.CW.WR.09] Passage: "The birthday candle burned down while everyone watched the empty chair." What craft move creates the emotional effect?{
~A. Explaining the entire family history#Incorrect. The history is not stated.
~B. Using a cheerful joke#Incorrect. The tone is not comic.
~C. Listing every guest#Incorrect. Guests are not listed.
=D. Focusing on an empty chair during a celebration#Correct. The contrast suggests absence or grief.
}

::CW_U01_L05_Q18::[MLA.CW.WR.08] Passage: "The birthday candle burned down while everyone watched the empty chair." Which evidence best supports the inference of absence?{
=A. everyone watched the empty chair#Correct. The focus on an empty chair supports absence.
~B. birthday candle#Incorrect. A candle alone suggests celebration.
~C. burned down#Incorrect. This shows time passing but not absence by itself.
~D. The passage is one sentence.#Incorrect. Sentence count is not evidence.
}

::CW_U01_L05_Q19::[MLA.CW.WR.10] Passage: "Nico packed three notebooks, two pens, and the photo he said he did not need." What inference is best supported?{
~A. Nico dislikes all notebooks.#Incorrect. He packs three notebooks.
=B. The photo matters more than Nico admits.#Correct. He packs it even though he says he does not need it.
~C. Nico forgot every pen.#Incorrect. He packs two pens.
~D. Nico has no feelings about the photo for sure.#Incorrect. Packing it suggests importance.
}

::CW_U01_L05_Q20::[MLA.CW.WR.08] Passage: "Nico packed three notebooks, two pens, and the photo he said he did not need." Which evidence supports the claim that the photo matters?{
~A. three notebooks#Incorrect. This does not show the photo matters.
~B. two pens#Incorrect. This is a supply detail.
=C. the photo he said he did not need#Correct. Keeping what he claims not to need suggests hidden importance.
~D. packed#Incorrect. The whole action matters, but the photo phrase is the strongest evidence.
}

::CW_U01_L05_Q21::[MLA.CW.WR.09] Passage: "Nico packed three notebooks, two pens, and the photo he said he did not need." Why is this a useful craft model?{
=A. It shows contradiction between what a character says and does.#Correct. Nico says he does not need the photo but packs it anyway.
~B. It teaches how to count school supplies only.#Incorrect. The supply list supports character meaning.
~C. It removes all character complexity.#Incorrect. The contradiction adds complexity.
~D. It explains the photo's full history.#Incorrect. The history is implied, not explained.
}

::CW_U01_L05_Q22::[MLA.CW.WR.10] Passage: "The dog waited by the gate every afternoon, even after the whistle stopped coming." What inference is best supported?{
~A. The dog is learning to cook.#Incorrect. Nothing supports cooking.
~B. The gate is brand new.#Incorrect. The passage does not say this.
=C. The dog expects someone who no longer arrives.#Correct. Waiting after the whistle stops suggests absence.
~D. The whistle is louder than ever.#Incorrect. The whistle stopped.
}

::CW_U01_L05_Q23::[MLA.CW.WR.08] Passage: "The dog waited by the gate every afternoon, even after the whistle stopped coming." Which detail supports the inference of loyalty or waiting?{
~A. dog#Incorrect. The dog alone does not prove loyalty.
~B. gate#Incorrect. The gate is setting, not the main evidence.
~C. stopped#Incorrect. The stopped whistle suggests absence, but repeated waiting is stronger evidence for loyalty.
=D. every afternoon#Correct. The repeated time shows ongoing waiting.
}

::CW_U01_L05_Q24::[MLA.CW.WR.09] Passage: "The dog waited by the gate every afternoon, even after the whistle stopped coming." Which writer choice implies backstory without explaining it?{
~A. The lack of punctuation#Incorrect. The sentence has punctuation.
=B. The stopped whistle#Correct. It suggests someone used to arrive or call but no longer does.
~C. A direct biography#Incorrect. No biography is given.
~D. A recipe format#Incorrect. There is no recipe.
}

::CW_U01_L05_Q25::[MLA.CW.WR.10] Which response best meets Lesson 5 mastery expectations?{
=A. "The detail of the hidden card suggests Mara is avoiding the apology because she places it where no one can see it."#Correct. This gives an inference and evidence.
~B. "The character is sad because I guessed it."#Incorrect. A mastery response needs evidence.
~C. "The passage means anything I want."#Incorrect. Inferences must be supported.
~D. "The passage has words, so it has meaning."#Incorrect. This is too vague.
}
